WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.524
Idea. Think of a car on a straight road. Its displacement, s, is how far it is from the start, in metres. The speedometer shows how fast that changes. This is the velocity, v.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:26.784
Now press the accelerator. The velocity changes. How fast the velocity changes is the acceleration, a. So acceleration is d v by d t. It is also the second derivative of s.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:34.516
So to go along this chain, from s to v to a, you differentiate. Each step along is one differentiation.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:43.483
Units matter too. Displacement is in metres, velocity in metres per second, and acceleration in metres per second squared.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:51.560
These questions give s or v as a formula in t. They ask for the velocity or acceleration at one time.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:01:04.893
Method. Step one: decide which quantity you are given and which you need. Step two: differentiate with respect to t, once or twice. Each step moves one place along the chain.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:13.533
Step three: now put the time into the new formula and write the answer with its unit. The trap is putting the time in too early.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:33.305
Worked example. June twenty nineteen. A particle P moves in a straight line. At time t seconds, its velocity v metres per second is t squared minus four t plus seven. Find the acceleration of P, in metres per second squared, when t is three. Two marks.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:39.799
Step one: we are given velocity, and we need acceleration. That is one differentiation.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:49.820
Step two: differentiate each term. T squared gives two t. Minus four t gives minus four. The seven is a constant, so it gives nought.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:59.633
A tempting route is to put t equals three straight into v. That gives four, which is the velocity, not the acceleration. Reject it.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:15.073
Step three: substitute t equals three into the acceleration. Two times three, minus four, is two. The unit is metres per second squared. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:24.257
Here is v against t. The gradient of this graph is the acceleration. At t equals three the gradient is two, which agrees.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:33.775
Common mistake. A common mistake is keeping the constant when differentiating. The derivative of seven is nought, not seven.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:55.855
Your turn. Now you try, from June twenty twenty two. A particle P moves along the x-axis. Its displacement is t to the power four, minus thirteen point five t, plus twelve. Find the velocity when t is three, and the acceleration when t is two. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:17.949
Answer. For velocity, differentiate the displacement once. T to the four gives four t cubed. Minus thirteen point five t gives minus thirteen point five. The twelve disappears. At t equals three, four times twenty seven, minus thirteen point five, is ninety four point five metres per second.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:29.943
For acceleration, differentiate the velocity again. Four t cubed gives twelve t squared. At t equals two, twelve times four is forty eight metres per second squared.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:41.063
The common wrong answer puts t equals three into x. That gives fifty two point five, which is a position. It is not the velocity, so it loses the marks.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:47.477
Remember: decide what you have and need, differentiate, substitute the time, and write the unit.
